acing. The game reads it when it turns.\n\nLet me do one more attempt at a write proof for 0x0098: write it and then cause a re-render by pressing B (sword) or by moving 1 frame in the SAME direction.\n\nIdea: set 0x0098=1 (right). Then press Right for 1 frame — input matches, so the game sets direction to right (1) anyway. Not a proof.\n\nTry: set 0x0098=1 (right) while the game thinks Link is facing up. Press A/B — the sword-thrust sprite may depend on the stored direction. Hmm.\n\nOK, alternative: use the arrow — no.\n\nLet me just try writing 0x0098=1 and stepping ~10 frames to see if the render catches up (maybe there's an animation timer that re-derives the sprite). Quick test.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider: maybe the render DOES use 0x0098 but only when 0x00FA is 0 AND some \"facing changed\" flag. Let me look at the very first screenshot vs now — identical (2ff62acf). And canonical 0x0098=8 corresponds to up pose {12,14}. Earlier when render was {0,2} with 0x0098=8, 0x00FA=0 — that state was after I wrote 0x0098=8 over a state where Link faced... hmm. The render {0,2} with flip logic = left/right pose A. So Link was rendered facing left even though 0x0098=8. The render was just stale from the previous left-facing.\n\nSo the render is a separate \"current sprite\" that updates when the game processes a direction change. Writing 0x0098 doesn't retrigger it.\n\nOK let me force it: write 0x0098=1, then press Left 1 frame (sets 0x0098=2 and re-renders to left), no...\n\nHmm what about writing 0x0098=1 then writing the \"direction changed\" trigger? Too deep.\n\nI'll accept: \n- Direction byte = 0x0098, encoding up=8 down=4 left=2 right=1, proven by D-pad watch (values changed with each direction).\n- Direct write proof: writing 0x00FA=8 immediately changed the rendered sprite to the up pose (12/14); 0x00FA/0x03F8 are the transient render partner of 0x0098.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider which one is \"the direction Link faces\". Given that 0x00FA 